course: dictionary
lesson: 2191
title: A place name with a meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are names of places, and they often hide a second job. «The Thames is about 346 km long; it rises in the Cotswold hills near Kemble, flows through Oxford, Reading and Windsor, and reaches the North Sea through London and a wide estuary.» The sentence is a list of places, so Cotswold is a place where a river starts, a bit like a starting point on a map.
explain: Now look at «Cotswold sheep» and «Cotswold stone». Here Cotswold is not the whole place, it is a describing word that tells you where something comes from. A Cotswold sheep comes from the Cotswolds, just as a Welsh cake comes from Wales. When a place name sits in front of another word, ask: what is this thing from?
explain: When you read a name you don't know, use the clues around it. The sentence mentions hills, a river and a town, so you can guess the Cotswolds are countryside, not a city. The dictionary confirms it: the Cotswolds are gentle hills and pretty villages in south-central England.
fact: The Cotswolds are a range of gentle hills and pretty villages in south-central England.
fact: The Cotswolds are famous for honey-coloured stone houses and sheep.
fact: «Cotswold» is also used as a describing word, as in «Cotswold stone» and «Cotswold villages», meaning from the Cotswolds.
fact: In the Middle Ages the Cotswolds grew rich on wool from Cotswold sheep, and that wool money built big churches.
fact: The Cotswolds are an Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, now called a National Landscape.
exercise: «The Thames rises in the Cotswold hills near Kemble.» Where does the River Thames start? | In the North Sea | In Oxford | In London | *In the Cotswold hills near Kemble
exercise: What does Cotswold mean in «Cotswold sheep»? | *From the Cotswolds | A type of wool made in Scotland | A very fast kind of sheep | A sheep that lives in a wood
exercise: What are the Cotswolds? | *A range of gentle hills and pretty villages in south-central England | A big river in London | A type of sheep | A big city in the north of Scotland
test: The Cotswolds are famous for houses built from stone. What colour is the stone? | Blue-grey like slate | *Honey-coloured | Pure white | Bright red
test: In the Middle Ages, what paid for big churches in the Cotswolds? | Gold from the Thames | Tourists from London | *Wool from Cotswold sheep | Oil from the North Sea
exam: Why did the Cotswolds grow rich in the Middle Ages? | *Wool from Cotswold sheep paid for big churches | The Thames flooded and made the land fertile | Ships from the North Sea brought them gold | Tourists from London built the villages
